Group Programming Project 1
  Mastermind is a two-player code-breaking game invented in 1970. 
The original game involved a sequence of four coloured pegs which would 
be arranged in secret by the Codemaker and deduced by the Codebreaker. 
This is a single player, number-based version of the game. The system 
generates a random four-digit number and the user inputs a four-digit guess. 
The program returns the number of digits that are correct but incorrectly 
placed and those that are correct and correctly placed. The user use this 
feedback to solve the systems generated code in 10 moves or less. 
  To use the user must simply enter their first four-digit guess. Once they 
guess correctly, or run out of moves, then can decide to play again or 
terminate the program. A running tally of wins and loses is kept between 
rounds. 
	We created a SequenceGenerator class with a generate() method to produce 
the secret code. The UserGuess class contains the input_guess() method that 
takes the users input and validates that it is four digits and that they are 
all integer values. Several data types were used in the program. Integers 
were used for counters for wins, losses, and guesses while strings were used 
to take user input and for validation of correct input. Boolean variables 
controlled the flow of the game like whether the user was out of turns or 
had guessed the code correctly. The primary data type for storage was lists.
Lists were used since they preserve order which allowed for position-based 
comparison. The special value None was used to mark out digits that had
already been matched to avoid over counting correct digits. 
   The code utilizes the standard ‘random’ library and the contained randint
function to generate the four digits of the system generated number. Other 
built-in functions like isdigit() were used for user input validation. 
Custom exception classes were defined to handle invalid input like non 
numerical characters or too many characters. 
	There are no issues we set out to solve that we were unable to do.
However, it would have been cool to use more sophisticated print 
statements to perhaps display the correct or incorrectly placed 
numbers represented by colours like Wordle. Another addition we could 
add is a difficulty parameter which could be used to change the amount
of digits in the target number and allowed digits in the users guess. 
For example if when we instantiate the target we did 
target_sequence = SequenceGenerator(1) 
it would generate a four digit number but
target_sequence = SequenceGenerator(2) 
would generate a five digit number. Since UserGuess is a child of 
SequenceGenerator it would be easy to carry this new parameter and use it 
to change the restrictions on the users input as well. 
